I think the restrictions should be based on realistic factors, and not on the
letter of the AD&D rules. It seems absolutely reasonable to me that a wizard
with a strength of 8 shouldn't be able to use a two-handed sword or halberd.
I can definitely understand how a lawful sword might refuse to serve a chaotic
character (whether it then "hits" its wielder or just act as a plain weapon is
another debate I don't want to start at the moment). It makes sense that a
barbarian should be much better at using, say, an axe than a wizard, because
the barbarian has a lot more experience with that weapon that the wizard (who,
most probably, had never actually held one in his hands before he found one in
the dungeon). So, I propose the following:

1. To each weapon is associated a "base strength". For each point of strength
   below this base strength, you get -1 to hit and -1 to damage, until you
   exceed, say, -4, at which time you are just told that the weapon is too heavy
   for you period (you are of course warned whenever you attempt to wield a
   weapon whose base strength is higher than your current strength, even if
   the penalty is only -1). The game should ensure that the initial weapon(s)
   you get are suitable for your strength (I don't think this would require
   any change given the present strength/weapon combinations for the various
   classes).

2. To each weapon class (dagger of any type, bow of any type, etc) is associated
   a "proficiency factor" showing how good the character is at this particular
   weapon. This could be an integer ranging from, say, -5 to +1, which would
   be used as a "to hit" bonus/penalty. The initial values should be 0 for any
   weapon you are initially given, or possibly +1 for the "best" weapon of
   fighter classes, 0 for their "alternate" weapon and -1 to -2 for
   traditionally "non-physical" classes like wizards and healers. That is, a
   fighter is expected to have a lot of experience with the weapons he brings
   with him, whereas a wizard might have used them only occasionally. The
   initial values for other weapon types should be -5 for everybody, or
   perhaps a random -5 to -3 for fighter classes (which might have used that
   weapon a couple times during their training). As you keep using a particular
   weapon, you gain experience with it and your proficiency increases (up to
   +1); you could for instance increase the proficiency factor by 1 after 10
   successful hits, or maybe use something not linear.

3. Aligned weapons should refuse to serve the "opposite" alignment, and perform
   less satisfactorily with non-coaligned characters (for instance a lawful
   character using a neutral sword might still get the damage bonus due to the
   fine qualities of the steel used to make the weapon, but its special
   properties like cold resistance or whatever would not apply).

Such a change would make it harder for people to wish for Mjollnir and get
the benefits right away - they would actually not get the electrical damage
unless they were coaligned, and it might take them time to learn to use the
weapon. Unfortunately it makes life harder for wizards et al, who will have to
rely on their other skills to survive (well, more so than presently). My
personal opinion on this matter (putting on my shield of reflection :-) ) is
that this is a good thing, because I don't like the notion that the one wizard
with whom I managed to win the game spent his time monster-bashing (AC -22,
+8 Excalibur) and never casting any spell because he had very little of those
- for some strange reason, after breaking open all the statues and buying all
the books he could find, opening all the chests, etc, he had only got 8
spellbooks, most of them fairly useless to him. Wands? Sure, he had some of
those, secret door location, opening and "of course" digging and wishing.
Basically the only magic he did was scrolls, but when the magic marker dried
out and still no scroll of charging was to be found anywhere, well, the only
magic that was left was ^T (used extensively :-) ).

On the other hand, the game should not become more difficult for a
particular character class, and you can't blame a wizard for turning into a
fighter if the dungeon has got no "attack" magic and spell use is severely
limited (in HARD mode). This problem can be solved (I think) by altering the
probability of finding an object of a particular class depending on the
character class (ie a wizard would find less weapons and more scrolls, etc).
This is not realistic at all, but I can't think of any better solution.

